iii. 	This whole writing is in connection with my Co-Founding of the MECE, 
Mythwrights for Ethical Creative Expression, a group recognizing the creative struggles 
the community faces with its art. Though the writing of this took place in the same 
timespan and was inspired by the same realization, this extended essay is written to be 
separate from the MECE and will, however, be featured as the main writing contained in 
their Center of Ideology. The essay and the group came about after knowledge reached 
me regarding the buying and selling of myths, and the portrayal of ownership of said 
bought myths. Seeing myths as art, I believe the selling of such art and the following split 
in creative direction from the original expression to be harmful to the art. Feels like 
when a series, movie series, or a game is given to another creative team. This will all 
be regarded later, mostly in section 4, on The Art of a Myth, and the following 
acknowledgments section. The later Addendum section (refer to the "Ethnography of 
the Myth Community, the Dawn of its Sixth Year of Dwindling Fate (Addenum) by Sematoc" 
book) could give perspective if read first, if you wish.

iv. 	DO NOT think I project myself as superior to others in any way; I want 
conversation and progress to be made. I must note my inexperience in writing; I didn’t 
take advanced English classes, so my grammar and diction will absolutely be flawed to 
more advanced readers. I’ll also note my relative newness to the community, the 
community is much older than I. This extended essay is very disconnected and doesn’t 
have a specific theme or idea throughout, largely reflecting the state of the community in 
different areas. Aside from that, I don’t use tools for rewording; I feel like what I say is 
too specific to be rightly corrected by an AI or other software. The wide range of topics is 
why it is split into sections, for you to read according to your interest. There’s also not a 
huge center for thought like this in the community; I won’t have too much evidence or 
previous thought to bring to the table. That being said, the essay will be more logic than 
ideology, at least that is what I wish for it. Overall, I wish to share my ideas of definition 
and reasons, the whats and whys, what has been and what will or should be. 
  I’ll start with the roots, what I believe a myth or hunter is, and the association with 
art within this community. Then, I’ll ask why we engage in making and hunting myths. 
I’ll then dive deep into the community itself, where it is right now, groups, positives, 
negatives, the history, and where others and I wish for it to go. Lastly, I’ll cover some of 
the plagues of the creators themselves, the myths themselves, whether doubtful 
thoughts or cheap progress, or deceitful motives. My main goal is to fully connect the 
myth community to art, and come to conclusions about unanswered questions, ideas that 
are rarely shared, and most importantly, call attention to the festering evils of the 
community. I believe that over the course of the extended essay, it shifts from ideology 
to analysis regarding the community. If you have ANY questions or corrections of any 
sort, feel free to notify me anywhere, @dinskard on Discord and SEMATOC on Roblox. I 
hope to respond to everybody and every inquiry. I’m being this open because most of 
this is subjective; I don’t have a lot of other perspectives close to me. I need you to also 
understand that none of what’s said here is a “wrong” perspective, just a unique one.
